  "Parts are unchanged from step 1.",
  "The SEN136B5B is modelled with the catalogue's ping-sensor type (GND, 5V, SIG), which matches its three pins.",
  "In the image the sensor sits in the half of the breadboard drawn at the top (rows f-j), which the catalogue calls bank 'b'. Its pins are in columns 9 (GND), 10 (5V) and 11 (SIG), and the wires use the same columns, so each wire shares a strip with its sensor pin. The exact row (j for the sensor, g for the wires) is read from the drawing.",
  "The black wire goes to the Uno GND pin beside AREF, which I took to be GND.1. Orange goes to 5V and blue to digital pin 7, as the circuit text and schematic say.",
  "The sensor is powered from 5V, inside its 5 V range. No resistor or LED is needed, and the tutorial has none."
